# MECE (core method)

**Mutually Exclusive** — at one level of a tree or list, items do not overlap; a fact or option
belongs in one bucket.  
**Collectively Exhaustive** — together they cover the decision space the team claims to cover; name
explicit exclusions rather than silent gaps.

## Handout A (course) — first cut

Authoritative cut menu: `PrimarySources/handouts/Handout-A-Three-Cuts-Same-Problem.md`

- Sketch **two or three** first cuts (A opportunity · B customer type · C business model) before committing.
- First-pass compare all three opportunities in hour 1 (**by 1:00**).
- **Product × customer = matrix** — never both on the same tree level.
- Define the four case criteria before scoring; give FDA status **one** home (readiness or barrier).
- Name what you left out (e.g. licensing, distributors) — case’s three opportunities are exhaustive only by case boundary.

## When MECE applies here

| Artifact | MECE test |
|---|---|
| Rival root frames | Different *locations* of the problem, not rewordings of one frame |
| Issue-tree branches (same level) | One cut rule; Handout A A/B/C — no product+customer on same level |
| Case options (SynELISA / NBS / custom) | Exclusive picks for “one opportunity”; no silent hybrid |
| Comparison criteria | business potential · readiness · barriers · speed to revenue — non-overlapping defs |
| Buyer / partner types (Handout A) | Research labs · pharma/CROs · CLIA labs · diagnostic developers · state NBS programs |
| Business models (Handout A) | Product sales · services/co-dev · partnership/evaluation |
| 90-day actions / metrics | No duplicate actions/metrics under new names |
| Killed alternatives | Appendix keeps choice set exhaustive vs case |

## Failure modes (call them out)

- **Overlap** — same evidence or option appears under two siblings
- **Gap** — a case option, buyer type, or criterion is missing with no stated exclusion
- **Mixed cuts** — one level mixes e.g. product *and* geography *and* “feasibility”
- **Action posing as a branch** — “do a pilot” is decision architecture, not a MECE cause branch
- **Double-duty row** — one line does two jobs (split it; failing MECE inside a row is a finding)

## Prepare vs decide

While frame/issue are **undecided**, use MECE to *test* starter lists and draft trees — tag
overlaps/gaps. Do not pretend an incomplete starter set is an exhaustive diagnosis.

Prompt-card tree critique tags (reuse): **overlap · gap · mixed cuts · action posing as a branch**.

## Output habit

Any tree, criteria set, or option list: state the **cut rule** in one sentence, then run MECE. If it
fails, fix or flag before treating it as structure for research.
